    "payload": {
      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMrs Edith Caldwell, 78, retired teacher.\nAttended as an urgent appointment, 31 August 2026.\nTelephoned the practice that morning and was seen within four hours, accompanied by her daughter.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nA five-day history of distorted central vision in the right eye.\nStraight lines — door frames and the lines on her knitting pattern — appear wavy.\nA new central grey patch noticed three days ago, now larger.\nNo pain, no redness, no flashes.\nNo change noticed in the left eye.\nShe has been knitting less this week because the pattern is hard to follow.\nShe has managed her usual daily tasks so far, relying on the left eye.\nShe noticed it when covering the left eye by chance while cleaning her glasses.\nNo similar episode before, and no previous treatment to either eye.\n\nVision:\nRight 6/24, having been 6/9 at her last sight test in July 2025.\nLeft 6/9. No improvement with pinhole on either side.\nNear vision right N18, left N6.\nSpectacles last updated in July 2025 and still comfortable for distance.\nReading addition plus 3.00 D.\n\nAmsler grid:\nRight eye — metamorphopsia through the central squares and a small absolute scotoma just superior to fixation.\nLeft eye — grid reported as straight and complete.\nShe had not used an Amsler grid before today.\n\nDilated fundus examination, right eye:\nSubretinal fluid at the macula with a small subretinal haemorrhage inferotemporal to the fovea.\nSoft confluent drusen and pigmentary change.\nNo optical coherence tomography available at this practice.\nOptic disc healthy. Peripheral retina flat.\n\nDilated fundus examination, left eye:\nScattered soft drusen and mild pigmentary change. No fluid, no haemorrhage.\nAppearance unchanged from the photographs taken in July 2025.\n\nAnterior segment:\nClear corneas. Mild nuclear sclerosis, not accounting for the acuity loss.\nIntraocular pressure right 16, left 15 mmHg.\nPupils equal and reactive, no relative afferent defect.\nLids and conjunctiva healthy.\nColour fundus photographs of both eyes taken today and sent with this letter.\n\nPast medical history:\nHypertension. Osteoarthritis of both knees.\nBlood pressure checked by her GP in June and reported as well controlled.\nRight hip replacement in 2019, with a good recovery.\nShingles on the left side of the chest in 2022, fully resolved.\nFlu and COVID vaccinations had last winter.\nWears hearing aids in both ears and hears well with them in.\nCarpal tunnel release on the left hand in 2016.\nAppendix removed as a child.\n\nMedication:\nAmlodipine 5 mg daily. Paracetamol as required.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSmoking:\nEx-smoker, stopped ten years ago after about thirty years.\n\nFamily history:\nHer mother had poor sight in old age; the cause was never known to the family.\nNo other family history of eye disease that she knows of.\n\nSocial:\nLives alone. Does not drive. A daughter can bring her to appointments at short notice.\nShe is a keen reader and is frightened of losing that.\nWidowed six years ago. Lives in a ground-floor flat and manages her own shopping and cooking.\nBelongs to a library reading group and knits for a local hospice.\nHer son lives in Canada and telephones every week.\nUses a bus pass and walks with a stick outdoors because of her knees.\nAsked whether her television licence would change if her sight worsened; given a sight loss charity's helpline number.\nHer daughter lives fifteen minutes away and works part-time.\nHas a cat and keeps a small garden herself.\nVolunteers at a charity shop on Tuesday mornings.\nEnjoys crosswords and radio drama.\nHer grandson set up a tablet for her, which she uses for video calls.\nAsked whether the practice could sell her a magnifier; this was left until the low-vision support is arranged.\nA friend from her reading group has had injections for a similar problem and found them bearable.\n\nPlan:\nUrgent referral under the fast-track macular pathway; she should be assessed within a week for anti-VEGF treatment.\nCounselled that treatment aims to preserve rather than restore vision and that delay costs vision.\nAn Amsler grid given for daily monitoring of the left eye, with instructions to report any change at once.\nDietary and smoking factors discussed. Low-vision support offered but deferred until after assessment.\nShe understood the plan, and her daughter took the hospital contact number.\nReferral sent by secure email the same afternoon, with the photographs attached.\nGiven the practice number to call if she has not heard from the hospital within three working days.\n",
      "recipient": "Dr Helen Marsh, Consultant Ophthalmologist, Medical Retina Service",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Refer this patient urgently for assessment of suspected neovascular age-related macular degeneration."
    },
    "guidanceNote": "Convey urgency clearly — emphasise the recent VA drop and metamorphopsia, but avoid copying every drusen detail unrelated to the wet AMD suspicion."
