    "payload": {
      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMr Anand Sharma, 52, taxi driver.\nSeen 30 August 2026.\nAttended at the end of a night shift; tired but alert and fully engaged.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nMild blurring of the right eye for about two weeks.\nOccasional floaters in the right eye, described as small dark threads, first noticed four days ago.\nThe floaters have not increased in number since they first appeared.\nNo pain, no field loss, no flashes.\nThe blurring is most noticeable when reading his phone and the fare meter, less so for distance.\nHe has noticed no change in the left eye.\nHe usually attends screening but has missed the last two appointments because of work.\nHis last screening result, about two years ago, showed background changes only and he was told to keep attending.\n\nVision:\nRight 6/12, left 6/9. Pinhole gives no improvement.\nHe wears single-vision distance glasses, last updated about three years ago, and uses ready readers for close work.\n\nDilated fundus examination, right eye:\nNew vessels at the disc, approximately one third of a disc area.\nScattered dot-and-blot haemorrhages in all quadrants.\nVenous beading in two quadrants.\nA small preretinal haemorrhage inferotemporally, which accounts for the floaters.\nNo tractional detachment seen.\nPeripheral retina examined with an indirect lens; no tear seen.\n\nDilated fundus examination, left eye:\nModerate non-proliferative changes — haemorrhages, microaneurysms and cotton wool spots.\nNo new vessels.\nNo venous beading seen in the left eye.\n\nMacula:\nNo clinically obvious oedema in either eye and no exudate within one disc diameter of fixation.\nOptical coherence tomography not available here, so macular oedema cannot be excluded.\n\nAnterior segment:\nClear. No iris new vessels seen on undilated examination.\nIntraocular pressure right 17, left 17 mmHg. Angles open.\nEarly nuclear lens changes both eyes, not affecting vision at present.\n\nPrevious eye history:\nNo previous laser and no injections in either eye.\nA stye on the left upper lid about five years ago settled with warm compresses.\n\nPast medical history:\nType 2 diabetes for fourteen years.\nLast HbA1c 84 mmol/mol.\nHe thinks this was measured at his GP surgery in the spring.\nHypertension, blood pressure today 156/94.\nHe says his readings are usually lower when checked at the surgery.\nRaised cholesterol, with no statin listed; lipid treatment needs GP review.\nLeft knee arthroscopy for a cartilage tear many years ago, fully recovered.\nMild hay fever in the summer months, no regular treatment.\n\nMedication:\nInsulin, twice daily mixed regimen.\nMetformin 1 g twice daily.\nRamipril 10 mg daily.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSocial:\nMarried with two teenage children; his wife works part-time in a school office.\nHe works night shifts five or six nights a week, which he says makes regular meals and glucose checks difficult.\nNon-smoker. Drinks alcohol rarely.\nHe plays badminton with friends most weekends.\nHis younger brother also has type 2 diabetes.\nHe supports the local cricket club and coaches junior players on Sunday mornings.\nHe is planning a family visit to relatives abroad at Christmas and asked whether he could fly; he was told the clinic would advise.\nFeet checked by the practice nurse last year, with no problems reported.\n\nPatient understanding:\nHe had believed that because his vision was still fairly good, his eyes were not at risk.\nHe now understands that the new vessels can bleed and that treatment aims to protect the sight he has.\nHe said he would rather lose some night vision than risk a bleed, and wants treatment as soon as possible.\nHe agreed to keep a record of his glucose readings to take to the GP review.\nHe asked whether the hospital has parking for the laser appointment and was given the clinic travel information.\n\nOccupational and licensing:\nHe drives a taxi and holds a local authority taxi licence, which applies Group 2 medical standards.\nHis best acuity, 6/9 in the left eye, is below the Group 2 standard; he was advised to stop taxi driving until reviewed and to contact the council licensing authority and the DVLA.\nExplained that pan-retinal photocoagulation can affect the visual field and night vision and that he may have licensing obligations; he was advised to notify the licensing authority and his employer, and this was documented.\nHe was visibly worried about his income and this was discussed openly.\nHis wife can drive him to and from the hospital, as the dilating drops will stop him driving afterwards.\n\nPlan:\nUrgent referral to the medical retina service, expected within two weeks, for likely pan-retinal photocoagulation.\nGlycaemic, blood pressure and lipid control emphasised, with a GP review requested.\nAny rapid tightening of glucose control should be coordinated with the retina service, as it can transiently worsen proliferative retinopathy.\nAdvised to return immediately if vision drops suddenly, as a vitreous haemorrhage is a real risk.\nFundus photographs of both eyes were taken today; copies are enclosed with this referral.\nA copy of this letter is being sent to his GP.\n",
      "recipient": "The Medical Retina Service, Diabetic Eye Clinic",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Refer this patient urgently for assessment of proliferative diabetic retinopathy requiring laser treatment."
    },
    "guidanceNote": "Disc new vessels make this proliferative and urgent — say so plainly. The driving occupation and poor glycaemic control are relevant to ongoing care and management."
