      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMrs Margaret Pryce, 74, retired librarian.\nAttended without appointment, 1 September 2026, 2.15 pm.\nShe phoned the practice late this morning after the second episode and was told to come in.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nTwo episodes of transient curtain-like loss of vision in the right eye over the past three days.\nEach lasted a few minutes and cleared completely.\nBetween episodes the vision has felt normal.\nThe second episode was this morning while she was gardening.\nShe described it as a dark blind coming down from above and then lifting again.\nNo episodes have affected the left eye.\nA new throbbing headache over the right temple for about ten days, unlike her usual headaches.\nHer usual headaches are mild tension-type headaches a few times a year, eased by rest.\nHer jaw aches after a few minutes of chewing and she has started leaving food.\nShe has eaten mainly soup and soft food for the past week because of this.\nThe scalp on the right is tender when she combs her hair.\nNo double vision and no drooping of either eyelid.\n\nVision today:\nRight 6/9, left 6/6. No loss at present.\nColour vision full on Ishihara both eyes.\nVisual fields full to confrontation in both eyes.\nShe wears varifocal glasses, last updated at this practice eighteen months ago.\n\nDiscs:\nNo swelling and no pallor at present in either eye.\nCup-disc ratio 0.2 both eyes; small crowded discs.\nDisc photographs taken today for comparison.\n\nPupils:\nNo relative afferent pupillary defect currently.\n\nAnterior segment and pressure:\nQuiet. Intraocular pressure right 14, left 15 mmHg.\nEarly lens changes in both eyes, not visually significant.\n\nTemporal arteries:\nRight temporal artery tender and thickened to palpation. Pulsation reduced compared with the left.\nLeft temporal artery not tender.\n\nSystemic symptoms:\nWeight loss of about 4 kg over two months, unintentional.\nShoulder and hip girdle stiffness for several weeks, worst in the morning and lasting over an hour.\nShe now pulls herself up the stairs on the rail and takes longer to dress in the mornings.\nNight sweats reported.\nNo cough, no breathlessness and no urinary symptoms.\nFeels feverish at times; temperature not measured.\n\nPrevious eye history:\nA minor corneal abrasion from a rose thorn three years ago healed within days.\nNo episodes of visual loss before this week.\n\nPast medical history:\nPolymyalgia-type symptoms, never formally investigated.\nShe mentioned them to her GP last year but did not go back when they eased for a while.\nOsteoporosis.\nDiagnosed after a wrist fracture from a fall on ice four years ago; the fracture healed well.\nRight hip replacement in 2019, with a good recovery.\nHer last breast screening mammogram was normal; she is no longer routinely invited.\n\nMedication:\nAlendronic acid weekly. Calcium and vitamin D daily.\nShe has taken both for four years without problems.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nInvestigations:\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein have not been done. No bloods available today.\nWeight 61 kg on the practice scales today.\n\nSocial:\nWidowed; lives alone in a bungalow with a small garden.\nHer husband died three years ago.\nIndependent in all daily activities and normally drives locally.\nHer daughter lives two hours away and was told about today's visit by phone.\nShe volunteers at the town library two mornings a week.\nShe grows vegetables on a shared allotment.\nHer neighbour, a retired nurse, has offered to stay with her at the hospital.\nShe asked whether her car could be left in the practice car park overnight; this was agreed.\n\nPatient understanding:\nShe understood the explanation and agreed to go straight to the hospital.\nShe said she had put the headache down to sleeping awkwardly.\nShe asked whether she could go home first for her nightclothes; she was advised to go directly, and the neighbour will bring her things later.\n\nPlan:\nUrgent same-day referral. I telephoned the acute medical take at 2.40 pm and she is being seen this afternoon.\nExplained that the aim is to prevent permanent loss of vision in either eye and that treatment is usually started before test results return.\nAdvised not to delay for any reason and not to drive herself; a neighbour is taking her.\nThe receiving clinician is asked to start high-dose corticosteroid therapy immediately, considering intravenous methylprednisolone because of the visual symptoms, without waiting for inflammatory markers or temporal artery biopsy.\nPlease involve ophthalmology the same day, as visual symptoms are present.\nAn embolic cause, such as carotid disease, should also be kept in mind for the curtain-like episodes.\nHer osteoporosis is noted as relevant to steroid treatment.\nA copy of this letter is going with her, and a copy to her GP.\n",
